  <section|Exact selected public path><label|exact-selected-public-path>

  The exact chart receipt is <verbatim|YM.MergeProof.East.FiresSky>: it gives
  panels <verbatim|P(s,i)> for <verbatim|i:Fin 3>, base arcs <verbatim|b(i)>,
  and a common check budget; each arc is <verbatim|b(i)+s>, while currency
  and angle do not change with <verbatim|s>. For every sufficiently large
  level and every <verbatim|s\<in\>[0,1]>, it supplies a witness <verbatim|Y>
  satisfying <verbatim|PoisedSky>. This is the output of
  <verbatim|YM.MergeProof.East.embalms> from the paid request; it is not yet
  the station-admissibility receipt.

  <verbatim|constructionRun> calls the native preselected postnumeric
  period-clock selector, which uses <verbatim|RG05Joint.plentifulWithPrediction>
  with the native funded prediction and price supply. In the selected Bardo
  construction, <verbatim|submits> produces the paid multi-scale chart
  request. <verbatim|embalms> uses the request and the later budget to obtain
  a delayed wait and a family of one-parameter chart receipts. The proof
  constructs the parameter-dependent charts by clipping a real parameter into
  <verbatim|[0,1]>; only each arc is shifted, while the currency and angular
  data remain fixed. Forward chart estimates and the simultaneous staging
  estimate supply the finite-level chart, invoice, legacy-cost, actuation,
  and stamp clauses. Bardo specializes that family to the actual funded
  configuration and passes it as the final <verbatim|FiresSky> family
  argument to <verbatim|Trace.Over.gang>.

  The nearby argument <verbatim|hS.costs.night> in that call is the separate
  <verbatim|Moonlit> receipt used by the nonlinear local construction; it is
  not the <verbatim|FiresSky> family. The latter is the specialized output
  <verbatim|hU> of <verbatim|embalms>. This distinction is needed to read the
  actual caller correctly.

  <section|Scale-selection derivation><label|scale-selection-derivation>

  For fixed base arc <verbatim|b>, the source defines <verbatim|gap A b k =
  a_k m_k^b>, with <verbatim|a_k\<gtr\>0> and <verbatim|m_k> the clock
  monitor. <verbatim|gap_rate> proves convergence to zero. The adjacent-scale
  estimate <verbatim|near_skies> proves eventually <verbatim|gap A b k
  \<less\>= gap A (b+1) (k+1)>. The manuscript's least-crossing argument then
  proves the interpolation statement: for small positive <verbatim|s>, choose
  the least <verbatim|k\<gtr\>J> with <verbatim|gap A b k \<less\>= s>;
  minimality and <verbatim|near_skies> place <verbatim|s> between
  <verbatim|gap A b k> and <verbatim|gap A (b+1) k>, and continuity in the
  arc parameter gives an offset in <verbatim|[0,1]>. The proof does not
  assume the requested scale is already a clock gap.

  <verbatim|YM.MergeProof.East.boatsSky> combines the chart family with the
  strict level curve. Its exact conclusion includes an index <verbatim|k>,
  level <verbatim|i>, base arc <verbatim|b>, price <verbatim|c>, and charts
  <verbatim|R>, with <verbatim|s k = Station.gap A b i>; the associated
  <verbatim|TimberSky> contains the level and reference-grade conditions,
  chart and invoice receipts for every <verbatim|N,p> with
  <verbatim|i\<less\>N>, a <verbatim|SeqPair> at <verbatim|costHint(l)>, and
  <verbatim|wok ... .toStation.Admissible> at rank <verbatim|l>, size
  <verbatim|sizeHint(l)>, and error <verbatim|costHint(l)>. Internally
  <verbatim|fliesSky> builds the strict curve from clock interpolation; the
  private <verbatim|await> step supplies the <verbatim|SeqPair>;
  <verbatim|podiumSky> supplies <verbatim|Granular> for the smooth table and
  real spin; and <verbatim|supply_wheel> combines it with
  <verbatim|F.Ordered>, point limits, and the reference hierarchy to obtain
  the station receipt. The quantitative three-panel estimate is the separate
  manuscript dependency <verbatim|sec:three-panel-weak-comparison>.

  <verbatim|YM.MergeProof.East.wakesSky> iterates the one-step conclusion
  grade by grade, requiring the next scale to be below its predecessor. It
  gives strict scale decrease, strictly increasing sequence indices, and
  levels tending to infinity. The manuscript proves the scalar interpolation
  and the elementary recursion from this exact one-step output; it does not
  identify the one-step output with <verbatim|FiresSky> alone.

  <section|Finite-cutoff limit and gallery
  assembly><label|finite-cutoff-limit-and-gallery-assembly>

  At each fixed cutoff, the lift bound puts the coefficient maps in a closed
  ball in a finite-dimensional product space. Compactness gives a proper
  subfilter limit, and maximality identifies it with the selected
  ultrafilter. Continuity passes reality and signed covariance; eventual unit
  identities pass by uniqueness of limits. For a fixed tuple of labels, the
  finite-volume measurement is a multilinear map depending on period and
  phase. Continuity of one varying functional is therefore not enough. The
  theorem <verbatim|calibrated_smear> first gives convergence on fixed
  basis-label tuples from <verbatim|F.Ordered>; <verbatim|basis_tuple>
  expands moving vectors in finite bases, and <verbatim|tendsto_multi>
  combines coordinate convergence with those fixed-basis moment limits. The
  arity and window hypotheses remain in force. The finite-level inequality
  passes by closedness, then its positive slack tends to zero.

  At growing cutoff <verbatim|d>, each fixed test eventually lies below the
  cutoff and inside the expanding window, while the paid error factor is
  <verbatim|1/(d+1)>. This proves tested approach for each fixed test. Finite
  separating tests give eventual injectivity at each fixed grade; the
  selected table dimension bound supplies the reverse inequality and hence
  surjectivity. The start of the isomorphism tail may depend on the grade.
  The source fills early indices grade by grade; it does not claim a uniform
  tail over all grades. Unit, reality, and signed covariance hold on each
  fixed grade's eventual isomorphism tail; arbitrary early isomorphisms need
  not preserve them. The separation input is the new all-grade free-detector
  chapter, not only the low-grade trace calculation.

  <verbatim|pays_sky> packages this scaling with its chart limits.
  <verbatim|gang> fixes the ultrafilter and reference Ward tensor and calls
  <verbatim|Store_l> in both its fixed-scale and arbitrary-null-sequence
  cases. <verbatim|Store_l> produces the full <verbatim|Scribe> record using
  the physical Marvel receipt and field-array inputs. Besides the chart and
  hierarchy clauses, the record includes: convergence of the recentered
  physical stress to the material tensor; for each table respecting the
  selected table, an eventual two-coordinate scalar decomposition of the
  rescaled energy tensor, with coefficients converging to the prescribed
  stress coefficient and zero; and convergence of the zoomed scalar
  observable to the prescribed unit multiple. These are actual separate
  source fields (<verbatim|Scribe.main>, <verbatim|Scribe.gallery>,
  <verbatim|Scribe.goal>), not consequences of the scale-selection proof. The
  correction, rank-one kernel, stable inverse, coefficient limit, and Ward
  normalization used in the handoff are treated in
  <verbatim|trace-calibration-producer.tex> only under its displayed
  receipts; this chart unit does not claim to derive those physical inputs.
  The metric/chart estimates used to construct the one-scale receipts remain
  assigned to their analytic source chapters.

